From 961f511f5dae3cd4edce696313d7d290c5f10e73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Certe Kim <18364439+CerteKim@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:09:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sc8180x-xiaomi-book-12.4: correct the
 backlight PWM frequency

The panel node declares the period the bootloader leaves in the PMC8180C
LPG: 851667 ns, which is 1174.2 Hz at the 511 step resolution the channel
is already using.  The vendor panel XML (PanelName PNC357DB1-4, returned
by the GPU0 _ROM method) asks for a 19200 Hz, 9-bit backlight PWM
instead, so the board has been running the backlight about 16x slower
than the firmware it was brought up by.

The period cannot simply be set to the vendor's value, because
pwm-qcom-lpg quantises it to

	resolution * pre_div * 2^M / refclk

with resolution {63, 511}, pre_div {1, 3, 5, 6}, M [0..7] and refclk
{1024, 32768, 19200000}.  19200 Hz is 1000 counts at 19.2 MHz and the
period field is only 6 or 9 bits wide, so the exact value is out of
reach: with 9-bit resolution the reachable periods below 100 us are
multiples of 26614.58 ns, i.e. 53230 ns (18786 Hz) or 79844 ns
(12524 Hz).

Ask for 53230 ns.  It resolves to 511 * 2 / 19200000 = 53230 ns, so
18786 Hz, 2.2% below the vendor's 19200 Hz.  Do not "fix" it to the
vendor's exact period: a request of 52083 ns selects the 6-bit
resolution instead and lands on 52500 ns (19048 Hz), which is 1.4%
closer in frequency for an eighth of the duty resolution.  Any request
of 53230 ns or more selects the 9-bit solution.

Verified on the board after installing the DTB:

  $ sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/pwm | grep -A2 backlight
   pwm-4   (backlight           ): requested
    requested configuration:  enabled, 53230/53230 ns, normal polarity
    actual configuration:     enabled, 53230/53230 ns, normal polarity

The second line comes back from lpg_pwm_get_state(), which recomputes the
period from LPG_SIZE_CLK_REG/LPG_PREDIV_CLK_REG, so the hardware is
running 18786 Hz.  max_brightness moving from 4095 to 3326 is the
expected consequence in pwm-backlight, whose level count is
DIV_ROUND_UP(period, fls(period)) capped at 4096.
---
 .../dts/qcom/sc8180x-xiaomi-book-12.4.dts     | 32 +++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x-xiaomi-book-12.4.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x-xiaomi-book-12.4.dts
index a87e06227f8a..3e769772a93b 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x-xiaomi-book-12.4.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x-xiaomi-book-12.4.dts
@@ -256,19 +256,39 @@
 
 	/*
 	 * Panel backlight: LPG channel 5 on the PMC8180C (register base 0xbd00,
-	 * PWM channel index 4).  The bootloader leaves it running at ~1.17 kHz,
-	 * 256 of 511 duty; nothing in Linux used to touch it, which is why the
-	 * brightness never changed.  Lowering/raising this duty clearly dims and
+	 * PWM channel index 4).  Lowering/raising the duty clearly dims and
 	 * brightens the panel, while the PMC8180C WLED block the panel used to
 	 * point at reads back as all zeroes and controls nothing.
 	 *
-	 * No brightness-levels table on purpose: pwm-backlight then uses its
-	 * CIE1931 curve with 4096 levels, which is a sane slider response.
+	 * The vendor panel XML (PanelName PNC357DB1-4, in the GPU0 _ROM) asks
+	 * for a 19200 Hz, 9-bit backlight PWM.  The bootloader instead leaves
+	 * the LPG at 851667 ns (1174.2 Hz, 256 of 511 duty), which is what this
+	 * node declared while nothing in Linux touched it - the reason the
+	 * brightness never changed.
+	 *
+	 * The period cannot be set freely: pwm-qcom-lpg quantises it to
+	 * resolution * pre_div * 2^M / refclk, with resolution {63, 511},
+	 * pre_div {1, 3, 5, 6}, M [0..7] and refclk {1024, 32768, 19200000}.
+	 * 53230 ns is the reachable value closest to the vendor's 19200 Hz that
+	 * still selects the 9-bit (511 step) resolution: it resolves to
+	 * 511 * 2 / 19200000 = 53230 ns = 18786 Hz.
+	 *
+	 * 19200 Hz itself is out of reach - it is 1000 counts at 19.2 MHz and
+	 * the period field is only 6 or 9 bits wide - and asking for its exact
+	 * period (52083 ns) is worse than useless: that lands on the 6-bit
+	 * resolution instead, giving 52500 ns (19048 Hz) at 63 duty steps, i.e.
+	 * 1.4% closer in frequency for an eighth of the duty resolution.
+	 *
+	 * No brightness-levels table on purpose: pwm-backlight then derives its
+	 * level count from the period and fills it with the CIE1931 curve, which
+	 * is a sane slider response.  The count is DIV_ROUND_UP(period,
+	 * fls(period)) capped at 4096, so it follows the period: 4096 levels at
+	 * the bootloader's 851667 ns, 3327 at this one.
 	 */
 	backlight: backlight {
 		compatible = "pwm-backlight";
 
-		pwms = <&pmc8180c_lpg 4 851667>;
+		pwms = <&pmc8180c_lpg 4 53230>;
 	};
 
 	pmic-glink {
